  "e.ch.current": "Current channel ψ",
  "e.ch.note": "★The channels advance <strong>together</strong> — one dt, one old state, the closure iterated over all of them: the heat capacity is (3/2)V′n, so a density that moves beside the temperatures moves the very weight they are solved with, and splitting them creates (3/2)V′T·dn of energy every step. ★<strong>The current channel needs a traced metric</strong>: it reads ⟨|∇ρ|²/R²⟩, which four scalars do not determine, so on Miller geometry the switch is <strong>disabled</strong> rather than quietly ignored.",
  "e.ch.momentum": "Momentum channel ω<sub>φ</sub>",
  "e.torque": "Torque T<sub>φ</sub> [N·m]",
  "e.prandtl": "Momentum Prandtl number χ<sub>φ</sub>/χ<sub>i</sub>",
  "e.mom_note": "★<strong>The momentum channel is not a fourth channel of <code>core_march</code></strong>, because in the kernel it is not one: heat, density and current advance together (the heat capacity moves with the density) and momentum is its own entry, <code>solve_momentum</code>. So this is an <strong>operator split</strong> — same dt, same old state, the χ<sub>i</sub> this step's closure produced. What the split drops is the feedback of rotation on the heat channels <strong>within</strong> a step; on this tier that feedback runs through the E×B shear, and the turbulent closure is re-evaluated on its own cadence anyway. ★<strong>χ<sub>φ</sub> is prescribed, not solved</strong>: a momentum diffusivity is a TGLF output and this tier's kernel entry does not carry upstream's toroidal-stress weights (<code>closure.momentum_chi_phi</code> refuses rather than returning a zero). So what you set is a <strong>Prandtl number</strong>, χ<sub>φ</sub> = Pr·χ<sub>i</sub>, and it travels in the file. ★<strong>The torque rides the same deposition profile as the auxiliary power</strong>: on this tier that power is a neutral beam, and a beam deposits both energy and momentum where it stops. A second pair of sliders would suggest the two can be set independently, and this tier has no ray tracing (that is T-M2). ★<strong>⟨R²⟩ is on this ladder now</strong> (T-M8): the momentum equation's capacity is V′ n m ⟨R²⟩, and while the ladder carried ⟨|∇ρ|²⟩ and ⟨|∇ρ|²/R²⟩ only, the capacity stood in the surface's own major radius squared, R<sub>maj</sub>(ρ)² — the first term of that average, short by O((a/R)²). Both metric carriers return the average itself now: the traced ladder from <code>equilibrium_ladder</code>'s own contour integrals, the Miller tier from <code>geo_surface</code>, against the same volume weight in both; on a circular surface the two differ by exactly 1.5 r² — measured on this page's EAST Miller surfaces, <strong>9.8 %</strong> at the edge, which moved ω(0) from 71.19 to 71.52 krad/s when it was wired in. <strong>Both columns travel in the file</strong> (<code>fylite:r2</code> and <code>fylite:rmaj2</code>) so the gap is yours to divide, and a metric without the column is <strong>refused</strong> rather than sent back to R<sub>maj</sub>². ★<strong>The edge is ω = 0</strong>: this tier has no scrape-off layer and no wall torque, so the boundary condition is \"the plasma is not rotating where the model stops\". ★<strong>And the E×B shear really does reach TGLF once it turns</strong>: <code>VEXB_SHEAR</code> comes from the kernel's <code>mapping::tglf_local</code> (which sign, which radius the derivative is against, which length normalises it, which sound speed — all four conventions live there), not respelled here. With the channel off the entry is 0, and that 0 is now a <strong>statement</strong> — no rotation was solved — rather than the placeholder it used to be.",
  "e.omega0": "ω<sub>φ</sub>(0) [krad/s]",
  "e.mach": "Axis Mach number R<sub>0</sub>ω/c<sub>s</sub>",
  "e.dt": "Time step dt [s]",
  "e.nsteps": "Steps",
  "e.dttarget": "Adaptive target change (0 = off)",
  "e.nlev": "Surfaces / grid points",
  "e.edgepsin": "Ladder outer edge ψ_N",
  "e.edgepsin_note": "★<strong>T-M13's trade-off lands here: the outer edge is a control, no longer a baked-in 0.95.</strong> The deposition shells run to ψ<sub>N</sub> = 1 while the ladder stops here — the beam power outside (measured ~1.1 % at the default) never reaches the march, and τ<sub>E</sub>, Q and the power balance are all short by that much; the calibre-gap row follows this control: push it toward 1 and the missing share shrinks. <strong>The slider ends at 0.99 — 1.0 is out of reach</strong>: the separatrix is not a metric surface this ladder can stand on (dV/dψ diverges logarithmically there on a diverted equilibrium; tracing ψ<sub>N</sub> = 1 is tracing the X point), so the gap is never pushed to zero, only onto the calibre you declared. The session file records the control.",
  "e.time_note": "★<strong>Zero keeps dt yours for the whole march</strong> — the arithmetic every case written before this field relies on, with <strong>one exception</strong>: while the heat channel is on the step is also capped by the <strong>electron-ion exchange time</strong> (a quarter of the fastest exchange time on the grid). The exchange reaches the operator explicitly, and a step longer than it does not blow up — it <strong>decouples the heat pair silently</strong> (measured: dt 0.13 s against an exchange time of 0.1 s reported T<sub>e</sub> = 11.8 keV beside T<sub>i</sub> = 6.3 keV with half the alpha power, while the change per step stayed small, which is exactly why the controller cannot see it). How many steps the cap bound, and what that time was, are <strong>reported in the readings</strong>. Non-zero adjusts it as dt ×= clamp(target/actual, ½, 2) after each step and turns a class of <strong>death into a slowdown</strong>: a state that comes back non-finite is thrown away, dt halved, the step retaken. ★The surface count is a <strong>request</strong>: a level whose contour will not close is dropped, and the axis node is this page's (V′(0) = 0, the flux-surface averages repeating their innermost traced value).",

