  "e.ser.ref_q": "reference q",
  "e.ref_note": "★You can <strong>import a reference profile set</strong> (CSV whose header carries rho / TE / TI / NE / q — an ASTRA output table, say). It is drawn dashed on the three profile plots and the peak and r.m.s. relative differences are reported per channel. The reference <strong>takes no part in the solve by default</strong>: it is not a fit target. ★Tick “start from the imported reference” and the march BEGINS on those profiles, holds that density and pins the edges to its own last point — the differences then measure how far this transport model drifts from the published solution, which is the <strong>reproduction test</strong> itself.",
  "e.ref_against": "★Measured against <strong>{name}</strong> (dashed).",
  "e.ref_dev": "Relative difference (peak / rms): {list}.",
  "e.p.label": "Pressure profile (hand to analysis)",
  "e.p.export_hint": "★Writes this march's <strong>total pressure p = (n<sub>e</sub>T<sub>e</sub> + n<sub>i</sub>T<sub>i</sub>)e</strong> as the document the analysis page reads (uniform psi_N grid), to be used there as the kinetic constraint — which is how a prediction gets reconciled with the magnetics. ★<strong>The grid does not reach the boundary</strong>: the metric is traced to psi_N = 0.95 and this bar has <strong>no answer</strong> beyond it, so from 0.95 to 1.0 the file <strong>holds</strong> the last <strong>solved</strong> value flat — a flat top, not a pedestal — and both facts travel as fields in the file. ★The provenance says <code>model-evolve-prediction</code>: this is a <strong>prediction</strong>, not a measurement and not a reconstruction output.",
  "e.r.label": "Reference profiles (CSV)",
  "e.r.no_export": "The reference is something this bar READS; it does not produce one.",
