  "e.pedestal": "Pedestal model (EPED1-NN solves the edge temperature)",
  "e.ped.note": "★<strong>The edge temperature is solved</strong>: EPED1-NN — the neural-network surrogate (Meneghini, NF 57 086034 (2017); weights from the open-source EPEDNN.jl, Apache-2.0, checksum pinned in the kernel) of EPED1 (Snyder, Phys. Plasmas 16 056118 (2009): peeling-ballooning stability + the KBM width Δ<sub>ψ</sub> = 0.076·√β<sub>p,ped</sub>, two constraints). Inputs are this page's machine size (a, R, κ, δ), B<sub>t</sub>, I<sub>p</sub>, Z<sub>eff</sub> and the edge density; β<sub>N</sub> feeds back from the march's own readings <strong>lagged one step</strong>; T<sub>ped</sub> = p<sub>ped</sub>/(2 n<sub>e,ped</sub>k) (T<sub>e</sub> = T<sub>i</sub>, EPED's own convention), and the two sliders above are disabled. ★The ladder stops at edgePsin while EPED's pedestal top sits at ψ<sub>N</sub> = 1−Δ — close but not the same point: this tier pins the Dirichlet point to the pedestal-top VALUE and does not model the pedestal <strong>interior</strong>. ★A machine outside the training box is answered anyway, with the extrapolation distance in the readings — the judgement is the reader's. A run holding published reference profiles keeps the reference's edge (this switch does not touch it).★★<strong>The shape and composition fed in are this page's convention, not the one EPED was validated on</strong> (T-C8): what goes in is the delta_95 / kappa_95 on the controls and the <strong>volume-average</strong> Z_eff, whereas the kernel's own ITER constant uses the <strong>boundary shape</strong> (delta = 0.485, kappa = 1.85) and the <strong>pedestal</strong> Z_eff = 1.8. Measured: same kernel, same n_ped, the two conventions differ by <strong>25 %</strong> in p_ped, and this page's puts T_ped below the published band the kernel's test requires. ★Also, <strong>n_ped is the edge-density control above</strong> — it sits at the ladder end, i.e. the pedestal top, so filling it with a separatrix value inflates T_ped = p/(2nk).",
